.TH STASH 1 "2026-05-06" "stash v1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
stash \- personal knowledge vault
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stash
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stash
captures, organizes, and searches URLs, text snippets, files, directories,
images, and email messages. All content is stored locally with full-text
search powered by SQLite FTS5.
.PP
Running
.B stash
with no arguments launches the interactive TUI.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B add \fI<url|file|dir|->\fR
Stash a URL, file, directory, or stdin snippet. URLs are fetched and
content is extracted automatically. Files are stored in a content-addressable
store and text is extracted when possible. Directories are automatically
archived as tar.gz before storing. Use \fB-\fR to read from stdin.
.TP
.B list
List stashed items, optionally filtered by type, tag, collection, or
date range.
.TP
.B search \fI<query>\fR
Full-text search across all stashed content.
.TP
.B show \fI<id>\fR
Show details of a stashed item. Accepts prefix IDs (minimum 6 characters).
Archive items (tar.gz, zip) display a tree listing of their contents.
.TP
.B edit \fI<id>\fR
Edit a stashed item's title, notes, tags, or extracted text.
.TP
.B delete \fI<id>\fR
Delete a stashed item. Prompts for confirmation unless \fB-y\fR is given.
.TP
.B open \fI<id>\fR
Open a stashed item in its default application.
.TP
.B link \fI<id1> <id2>\fR
Create a link between two items. Use \fB-l\fR to add a label
and \fB--directed\fR for a one-way link.
.TP
.B unlink \fI<id1> <id2>\fR
Remove a link between two items.
.TP
.B tag list
List all tags.
.TP
.B tag rename \fI<old> <new>\fR
Rename a tag across all items.
.TP
.B tag graph
Show tag co-occurrence data. Each pair of tags that appear together
on items is listed with a weight (number of shared items).
.TP
.B import bookmarks \fI<file>\fR
Import bookmarks from a Chrome or Firefox HTML export. Folders become
tags. Duplicate URLs are skipped. Use \fB--dry-run\fR to preview.
.TP
.B collection list
List all collections.
.TP
.B collection create \fI<name>\fR
Create a new collection. Use \fB-d\fR to set a description.
.TP
.B collection show \fI<name>\fR
Show items in a collection.
.TP
.B collection delete \fI<name>\fR
Delete a collection (items are retained).
.TP
.B rules list
List configured capture rules from \fI$STASH_DIR/rules.yaml\fR.
.TP
.B rules test \fI<id>\fR
Show which rules would apply to an existing item — matched rules, tags
that would be added, collection assignment, title overrides, note
updates, link targets, notify messages, and whether the item would be
skipped. No writes.
.TP
.B rules apply \fR[\fB--dry-run\fR] [\fB--type \fItype\fR] [\fB--tag \fItag\fR] [\fB--rule \fIname\fR]
Retroactively run rules over existing items. Tags, collection, title, and
note actions apply; \fIskip\fR and \fInotify\fR are capture-time only and
do nothing on retroactive runs. Use \fB--dry-run\fR to preview changes,
\fB--type\fR/\fB--tag\fR to filter the candidate set, or \fB--rule\fR to
apply a single named rule. Items that already have any collection are
not reassigned, mirroring the precedence rule used by \fBstash add\fR.
.TP
.B rules enable \fI<name>\fR / rules disable \fI<name>\fR
Toggle a rule's \fIenabled\fR flag. The rule remains in the file when
disabled but does not fire. Comments at the top of the file are preserved
across toggles; YAML indentation may reflow.
.TP
.B rules save
Read a single rule as JSON from stdin and upsert it into the rules file
(replace if the name exists, append otherwise). Used by the Mac app's
rule editor. Comments and other rules are preserved.
.TP
.B rules remove \fI<name>\fR
Delete a rule by name from the rules file.
.TP
.B rules log \fR[\fB--type \fItype\fR] [\fB--rule \fIname\fR] [\fB--limit \fIN\fR] [\fB--since \fIduration\fR] [\fB--tail\fR]
Show recent rule activity from \fI$STASH_DIR/rules.log\fR. Three event
types: \fBfire\fR (capture matched ≥1 rule and was saved), \fBskip\fR
(rule with skip:true matched and dropped the capture), and \fBretro\fR
(\fBrules apply\fR changed an existing item). Newest first.
\fB--type\fR / \fB--rule\fR filter the result; \fB--limit\fR caps the
count (default 50, 0 for all); \fB--since\fR accepts \fB30m\fR /
\fB1h\fR / \fB7d\fR / \fB1w\fR style values; \fB--tail\fR (\fB-f\fR)
follows the log live, streaming new events as they arrive (Ctrl-C to
stop). \fB--json\fR emits one Event per line in the on-disk JSONL shape
for piping through \fBjq\fR.
.SS Rule actions
Each rule has match conditions plus a list of actions. Action types:
.B add_tags
(additive across rules),
.B add_collection
(first-match-wins, auto-creates if missing),
.B set_title
(template; first-match-wins; overrides extracted title unless \fB-t\fR flag was passed),
.B set_note
(template; first-match-wins),
.B append_note
(template; stacks across rules; newline-separated),
.B skip
(boolean; if any matched rule sets it, the item is logged to
\fI$STASH_DIR/skip.log\fR and not saved),
.B notify
(template; fires a macOS desktop notification via osascript),
.B link_to
(\fItag\fR or \fIid\fR; auto-links the new item to the matched targets,
capped at 50 to prevent runaway fanout).
.PP
Templates support these variables:
\fB{{.Title}}\fR, \fB{{.URL}}\fR, \fB{{.Domain}}\fR, \fB{{.Type}}\fR,
\fB{{.MimeType}}\fR, \fB{{.Sender}}\fR, \fB{{.SenderName}}\fR,
\fB{{.SenderEmail}}\fR, \fB{{.SenderDomain}}\fR, \fB{{.Subject}}\fR,
\fB{{.Filename}}\fR, \fB{{.Date}}\fR, \fB{{.Rule.Name}}\fR.
.PP
Named regex capture groups in \fIurl_regex\fR / \fIcontent_regex\fR are
exposed as \fB{{.Captures.\fIname\fR}}\fR. For example,
\fIcontent_regex: "Total:\\s*(?P<amount>\\$[0-9.]+)"\fR + \fIset_title:
"Invoice {{.Captures.amount}}"\fR. Missing names render as empty strings.
.SS Notifications
The \fInotify\fR action fires a desktop notification using a
platform-appropriate backend.
.PP
On macOS, if \fBterminal-notifier\fR is installed (\fIbrew install
terminal-notifier\fR), notifications are clickable — clicking the banner
opens the item's URL (link items) or source file (file items) in its
default app. Without \fBterminal-notifier\fR, notifications fall back to
\fIosascript\fR's \fBdisplay notification\fR, which is non-clickable.
.PP
On Linux, the action uses \fBnotify-send\fR (libnotify). On other
platforms it logs to stderr.
.SS Migration
On first read, if \fI$STASH_DIR/rules.yaml\fR does not exist but
\fI$STASH_DIR/autotag.yaml\fR does, the legacy file is silently migrated
to the new \fIactions:\fR list shape and renamed.
.TP
.B check
Check stash for data hygiene issues. Reports broken URLs, missing files,
orphaned files, and duplicate content. Use \fB--urls\fR, \fB--files\fR, or
\fB--dupes\fR to limit the scan. Use \fB--stream\fR to emit newline-delimited
JSON events as findings arrive (instead of a single summary at the end);
useful for progressive UI rendering.
.TP
.B stats
Show stash statistics: counts by type, top tags, monthly growth, storage usage.
.TP
.B refresh \fI<id>\fR
Re-fetch URL content for the given item, updating its title, extracted text,
and last-fetched timestamp.
.TP
.B backup
Create a backup of the stash database and content files.
.TP
.B restore \fI<path>\fR
Restore the stash from a previously created backup.
.TP
.B ui\fR, \fBtui
Launch the interactive terminal UI.
.TP
.B man
Display this manual page.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global Flags
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-db \fI<path>\fR
Custom database path.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version.
.SS Add Flags
.TP
.B \-t\fR, \fB\-\-title \fI<title>\fR
Set a custom title (auto-detected if omitted).
.TP
.B \-T\fR, \fB\-\-tag \fI<tag>\fR
Add tags (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-n\fR, \fB\-\-note \fI<note>\fR
Attach a note.
.TP
.B \-c\fR, \fB\-\-collection \fI<name>\fR
Add to a collection.
.TP
.B \-\-type \fI<type>\fR
Force item type: link, snippet, file, image, email.
.TP
.B \-d\fR, \fB\-\-delete
Delete the source file or directory after a successful stash.
Only applies to file and directory sources, not URLs or stdin.
.SS Link Flags
.TP
.B \-l\fR, \fB\-\-label \fI<label>\fR
Add a label to the link.
.TP
.B \-\-directed
Create a directed link (from id1 to id2).
.SS Edit Flags
.TP
.B \-t\fR, \fB\-\-title \fI<title>\fR
Set a new title.
.TP
.B \-n\fR, \fB\-\-note \fI<note>\fR
Set a new note.
.TP
.B \-e\fR, \fB\-\-extracted\-text \fI<text>\fR
Set the extracted text content.
.TP
.B \-\-add\-tag \fI<tag>\fR
Add a tag (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-tag \fI<tag>\fR
Remove a tag (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-c\fR, \fB\-\-collection \fI<name>\fR
Add to a collection.
.SS Filter Flags (list, search)
.TP
.B \-\-type \fI<type>\fR
Filter by item type.
.TP
.B \-\-tag \fI<tag>\fR
Filter by tag.
.TP
.B \-\-collection \fI<name>\fR
Filter by collection.
.TP
.B \-\-after \fI<date>\fR
Show items created after this date.
.TP
.B \-\-before \fI<date>\fR
Show items created before this date.
.TP
.B \-l\fR, \fB\-\-limit \fI<n>\fR
Maximum number of results (default 50).
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
The TUI supports the following keys:
.TP
.B /
Open search. Supports \fBtag:\fIname\fR filter syntax.
.TP
.B 1\-5
Toggle type filter (1=urls, 2=snippets, 3=files, 4=images, 5=emails).
.TP
.B j/k \fRor\fB Up/Down
Navigate items.
.TP
.B Enter
View item detail.
.TP
.B b
Open file browser for browsing, selecting, and batch stashing files and directories.
Supports multi-select (space), select all (a), open (o), and directory navigation (h/l or arrows).
.TP
.B g
Tag graph \(em browse tags and their co-occurrence connections. Navigate
with j/k, press Enter to filter the item list by the selected tag.
.TP
.B o
Open item in default application. Archives are extracted to a temp directory.
.TP
.B d
Delete item (prompts for y/n confirmation).
.TP
.B l
Link current item to another (search and select).
.TP
.B u
Unlink a linked item (select from list).
.TP
.B r
Refresh item list.
.TP
.B ?
Show help overlay with all keyboard shortcuts.
.TP
.B q
Quit (list view) or go back (detail view).
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Force quit.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Clear search and filters.
.SH ITEM TYPES
.TP
.B link
Bookmarked URLs. Content is fetched and extracted via readability.
.TP
.B snippet
Text captured from stdin.
.TP
.B file
Local files stored in the content-addressable file store.
.TP
.B image
Image files with metadata extraction.
.TP
.B email
RFC 2822 email messages (.eml files) with header and body extraction.
.SH ORGANIZING: TAGS VS COLLECTIONS VS LINKS
Stash offers three ways to organize items, each serving a different purpose.
.SS Tags \(em What is it about?
Flat labels describing the content's topic or nature. An item can have many
tags. Use tags for filtering, searching, and cross-cutting categories.
Tags answer \fBshow me everything about X\fR.
.SS Collections \(em Where does it belong?
Named groups, like folders or projects. An item belongs to one collection
(or none). Use collections for grouping items by purpose, project, or workflow.
Collections answer \fBshow me everything for project Y\fR.
.SS Links \(em How does it relate to other items?
Direct relationships between specific items. Bidirectional or directed, with
optional labels. Use links for connecting a source to its reference, or grouping
related items that aren't topically similar.
Links answer \fBwhat's connected to this specific item?\fR.
.SS Tag Graph
The tag graph (\fBg\fR in TUI, or Tag Graph in the sidebar) reveals how your
topics cluster. Tags that frequently co-occur on the same items show your
knowledge areas and how they overlap.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is read from \fI~/.config/stash/config.toml\fR:
.PP
.nf
data_dir     = "~/.stash"
db_path      = "~/.stash/stash.db"
files_dir    = "~/.stash/files"
image_viewer = ""  # custom image viewer command
.fi
.PP
Precedence: CLI flags > \fBSTASH_DIR\fR env var > config file > defaults.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/stash/config.toml
Configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.stash/stash.db
SQLite database with FTS5 full-text search.
.TP
.I ~/.stash/files/
Content-addressable file store (SHA-256).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B STASH_DIR
Override the data directory (default: \fI~/.stash\fR).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error.
